### What is a Java Agent?

A Java Agent is a library loaded through the `java.lang.instrument` package that can transform class bytecode in the window between the JVM reading the class bytes and the class being defined. The running application sees the modified version as if it had always been that way.

```java
package com.bank.agent;

import java.lang.instrument.Instrumentation;

public class BankTimingAgent {

    // Called before the application's main method
    public static void premain(
            String agentArgs, Instrumentation inst) {
        System.out.println("Bank Timing Agent loaded");
        inst.addTransformer(new TransferTimingTransformer());
    }
}
```

### How does the premain vs agentmain attachment work?

The agent jar has a manifest declaring entry points. The `-javaagent` flag triggers `premain` at startup. The attach API triggers `agentmain` on a running JVM.

```text
# MANIFEST.MF for startup agent
Premain-Class: com.bank.agent.BankTimingAgent
Can-Retransform-Classes: true

# MANIFEST.MF for dynamic agent
Agent-Class: com.bank.agent.BankTimingAgent
Can-Retransform-Classes: true
```

```bash
# Startup attachment
java -javaagent:bank-timing-agent.jar=verbose \
     -jar bank-transfer-service.jar

# Dynamic attachment to running JVM (PID 12345)
# Using the Attach API programmatically
```

```java
package com.bank.agent;

import java.lang.instrument.Instrumentation;

public class BankTimingAgent {

    // Startup: called before main
    public static void premain(
            String agentArgs, Instrumentation inst) {
        System.out.println("Startup agent: " + agentArgs);
        inst.addTransformer(
            new TransferTimingTransformer(), true);
    }

    // Dynamic: called when attached to running JVM
    public static void agentmain(
            String agentArgs, Instrumentation inst) {
        System.out.println("Dynamic agent: " + agentArgs);
        inst.addTransformer(
            new TransferTimingTransformer(), true);
        // Re-instrument already-loaded classes
        try {
            for (Class<?> cls : inst.getAllLoadedClasses()) {
                if (cls.getName().startsWith("com.bank")) {
                    inst.retransformClasses(cls);
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

### How does ClassFileTransformer work?

The `Instrumentation` object's `addTransformer` method registers a callback the JVM invokes for every class as it loads. The transformer receives the raw bytes and returns null (unchanged) or modified bytes.

```java
package com.bank.agent;

import java.lang.instrument.ClassFileTransformer;
import java.security.ProtectionDomain;

public class TransferTimingTransformer
        implements ClassFileTransformer {

    @Override
    public byte[] transform(
            ClassLoader loader,
            String className,
            Class<?> classBeingRedefined,
            ProtectionDomain protectionDomain,
            byte[] classfileBuffer) {

        // Only instrument bank service classes
        if (className == null
                || !className.startsWith("com/bank/service")) {
            return null; // Leave unchanged
        }

        System.out.println("Instrumenting: " + className);

        // Use ByteBuddy or ASM to modify classfileBuffer
        // and return modified bytes
        return instrumentWithTiming(classfileBuffer);
    }

    private byte[] instrumentWithTiming(byte[] original) {
        // In practice, use ByteBuddy or ASM here
        // to add timing probes at method entry/exit
        return original; // Simplified
    }
}
```

### How do bytecode libraries help? (ASM, ByteBuddy, Javassist)

Hand-writing raw bytecode is risky, so agents use libraries. ASM is low-level and fast. Javassist offers a source-like API. ByteBuddy is modern and fluent, used by OpenTelemetry's Java agent.

```java
package com.bank.agent;

import net.bytebuddy.agent.builder.AgentBuilder;
import net.bytebuddy.asm.Advice;
import net.bytebuddy.matcher.ElementMatchers;
import java.lang.instrument.Instrumentation;

public class ByteBuddyTimingAgent {

    public static void premain(
            String args, Instrumentation inst) {
        new AgentBuilder.Default()
            .type(ElementMatchers.nameStartsWith("com.bank.service"))
            .transform((builder, type, classLoader, module, domain) ->
                builder.method(ElementMatchers.any())
                    .intercept(Advice.to(TimingAdvice.class))
            )
            .installOn(inst);
    }

    public static class TimingAdvice {

        @Advice.OnMethodEnter
        static long enter() {
            return System.nanoTime();
        }

        @Advice.OnMethodExit
        static void exit(
                @Advice.Enter long startTime,
                @Advice.Origin String method) {
            long elapsed = System.nanoTime() - startTime;
            if (elapsed > 1_000_000) { // > 1ms
                System.out.println(method + " took "
                    + elapsed / 1_000_000 + "ms");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### What real tools are built on Java Agents?

| Tool | What It Does | Agent Technique |
|---|---|---|
| New Relic / Dynatrace | APM: latency, traces, errors | Instruments frameworks and drivers at load time |
| JaCoCo | Code coverage | Inserts probes recording line and branch execution |
| OpenTelemetry | Distributed tracing + metrics | ByteBuddy-based auto-instrumentation of 100+ libraries |
| JRebel | Hot-reload | Reloads changed classes into running JVM |
| Mockito (inline) | Mock creation | Instruments classes for testing |

### How does retransformClasses work?

`Instrumentation.retransformClasses()` re-instruments already-loaded classes. This is how dynamic attachment (agentmain) can add behavior to classes that loaded before the agent was attached.

```java
// After dynamic attachment, re-instrument TransferService
inst.retransformClasses(
    Class.forName("com.bank.service.TransferService")
);
// The transformer's transform() is called again
// for this class, even though it was already loaded
```

### What are common mistakes with agents?

The first mistake is instrumenting core JDK classes loaded very early, which can crash the JVM. The second is not testing the transformer thoroughly, because corrupted bytecode crashes the JVM.

```java
// WRONG: instrumenting everything including JDK classes
public byte[] transform(...) {
    return instrumentWithTiming(classfileBuffer); // Dangerous!
}

// CORRECT: filter to application classes only
public byte[] transform(...) {
    if (className == null
            || !className.startsWith("com/bank/")) {
        return null; // Leave JDK classes untouched
    }
    return instrumentWithTiming(classfileBuffer);
}
```

### What about native images and GraalVM?

Because agents rewrite bytecode at load time, they do not fit the closed-world native-image model where all classes are known at build time. Native-image ecosystems provide build-time instrumentation alternatives instead.

### What should a senior engineer know?

Instrumentation has real power and real responsibility: a buggy transformer can corrupt bytecode and crash the JVM, so agents use battle-tested libraries and are carefully tested. There is load-time and run-time overhead to keep small and configurable. Order matters when multiple agents transform the same class, since they chain. Dynamic attachment to production JVMs is powerful but is a security consideration, so it can be restricted.
